<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Urban VIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1568">1568</a>–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Maffeo Barberini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Urban VIII, born Maffeo Barberini, was pope from 1623 to 1644. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Bernini</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> art, he enriched his nephews on a large scale and presided over the trial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> in 1633. His war against the Farnese over Castro, begun in 1641, drained papal finances and ended in failure shortly before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii.jpg" alt="Pope Urban VIII" width="320" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro da Cortona · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_da_Cortona_-_Portrait_of_Urban_VIII_(ca._1624-1627)_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maffeo Vincenzo Barberini was born in April 1568 at Barberino Val d'Elsa, the son of the Florentine nobleman Antonio Barberini and Camilla Barbadoro. His father died when he was three, and his mother took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where his uncle Francesco Barberini, an apostolic protonotary, took charge of him. He was educated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and received a doctorate of law from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> in 1589. He served as papal legate to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> from 1601, became Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> in 1604, and was made a cardinal by Pope Paul V in 1606.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Barberini was elected pope on 6 August 1623 after the death of Gregory XV and took the name Urban VIII. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> covered 21 years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. His military policy aimed less at restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> than at securing his independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Duchy of Urbino was incorporated into the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> in 1626, and in 1641 he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-castro">Wars of Castro</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoardo-farnese-duke-of-parma">Odoardo Farnese, Duke of Parma</a> and Piacenza, whom he excommunicated. Castro was destroyed and its duchy joined the Papal States; he was the last pope to extend papal territory.</p><p>He opposed Copernican heliocentrism and ordered the trial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> after the Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems appeared, in which the character Simplicio argues Urban's view. Galileo was summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1633. Urban created 74 cardinals, among them his brother and two nephews, and his family amassed great wealth; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-von-ranke">Leopold von Ranke</a> estimated 105 million scudi. His 1638 bull Commissum Nobis forbade the enslavement of natives living in the Jesuit Reductions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Urban was a patron of the arts and favoured the sculptor and architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Gian Lorenzo Bernini</a>, who worked on the Palazzo Barberini, the baldacchino in St Peter's Basilica and the Fontana del Tritone. Bronze taken from the portico of the Pantheon prompted the saying that what the barbarians did not do, the Barberini did.</p><p>The papal debt rose from 16 million scudi at his election to 28 million by 1635 and 35 million by 1640. The cost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-castro">Wars of Castro</a> made him unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and a crowd destroyed his bust on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> after his death on 29 July 1644. His death is said to have been hastened by distress at the war's outcome. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-x">Giovanni Battista Pamphili</a> succeeded him as Innocent X.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Urban VIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then fought the First War of Castro against <b>Pope Urban VIII</b> in 1641–1644 over the family fief north of Rome; in a second war in 1649 Pope Innocent X captured Castro and razed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Two forms of the words are in use: the original ninth-century text, kept by some Protestant churches and certain Catholic religious orders, and a classicised revision published in 1632 under <b>Pope Urban VIII</b>, used in most Catholic parishes.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li><li><blockquote>A quarrel over these debts and the Farnese's power led <b>Pope Urban VIII</b> to fight the First War of Castro in 1641–1644, which ended in a papal setback.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-castro">Duchy of Castro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Urban VIII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
